== 1. RE-VERIFICATION (live docs, fetched 2026-09-14) ==

CEREBRAS — the constraint HAS SHIFTED since the 2026-09-01 matrix.
  inference-docs.cerebras.ai/capabilities/structured-outputs and /resources/openai now say:
  "Support for combining tools and response_format is model-dependent. Do not combine them
  unless the selected model's documentation explicitly supports and validates the combination."
  AND explicitly: "gpt-oss-120b rejects requests containing both fields."
  => No longer framed as an engine-wide hard-XOR; it is now PER-MODEL. BUT both models this
  engine serves reject it: gpt-oss-120b (our default) and zai-glm-4.7. No Cerebras model is
  currently documented as allowing the combination. So today the reject holds for 100% of
  Cerebras models; the docs merely reserve the right to lift it per-model later.
  The reject covers response_format of EITHER type (json_object AND json_schema) + tools.

GROQ — still true, with two nuances the ticket did not capture.
  console.groq.com/docs/structured-outputs: "Streaming and tool use are not currently
  supported with Structured Outputs." Confirmed 2026-09-14.
  Nuance A: "Structured Outputs" == response_format type json_schema (strict OR best-effort).
  json_object mode is a SEPARATE feature and is NOT restricted — json_object + tools is fine.
  A naive "tools + response_format" croak therefore OVER-fires on Groq json_object.
  Nuance B: it is genuinely THREE-way: json_schema XOR tools, and json_schema XOR streaming.
  The streaming leg bites chat_stream_realtime_f, not just chat_f.
  Also per-model: only gpt-oss-20b/120b, qwen-3.8-27b (strict) + safety-gpt-oss-20b
  (best-effort) do json_schema at all; other Groq models can only do json_object.

== 2. SCOPE — is it 2 or 5? Answer: CONFIRMED 2 (Cerebras + Groq). Do NOT build for 5. ==

Precondition for chat_f to build a conflicting body = engine advertises BOTH tools_native AND
response_format_json_schema. Walked the whole OpenAI-family Tools fleet:

  CONFIRMED reject (build for these):   Cerebras, Groq
  Provider SUPPORTS the combo:          OpenAI ("you can use both ... at the same time - on
                                        Chat Completions", verified). NOT affected.
  Pair ALREADY neutralised by k138:     DeepSeek (clears json_schema; json_object+tools is
                                        provider-allowed), MiniMax (clears both response_format),
                                        Hetzner (clears tools_native + both response_format).
                                        chat_f cannot form the pair -> no bug.
  No documented API-level exclusion:    Mistral, XAI/Grok, Moonshot/Kimi, Scaleway
                                        (searched 2026-09-14; treat as not-affected until a
                                        live 400 says otherwise — verify-don't-assert).
  Meta-aggregators (transitive risk):   OpenRouter, HuggingFace, Replicate, TSystems, AKIOpenAI.
                                        These pass through to a backend and CAN hit the reject
                                        when routed to gpt-oss-120b / a Cerebras or Groq-style
                                        stack (TSystems AND AKIOpenAI both DEFAULT to
                                        gpt-oss-120b). But that is a per-ROUTE / per-MODEL
                                        property, not an engine property, and cannot be honestly
                                        encoded as an engine-wide rule. Record as a known limit.

LOAD-BEARING INSIGHT: the exclusion is fundamentally a MODEL property (gpt-oss-120b + a few
constrained-decoding stacks), not an engine one — which is exactly why Cerebras made it
model-dependent and why the same model rejects on Cerebras, TSystems and AKIOpenAI. This is the
same per-model axis k138 (ADR 0019) opened. The catch: ADR 0019 explicitly said the boolean
model_capability_corrections table must NOT be stretched to fake a pairwise constraint (that is
literally k142's Future-work entry).

== 3. SHAPE RANKING ==

(a) PER-ENGINE CROAK on the unsupported combination  —  RECOMMENDED interim minimum.
  ADR 0002: does NOT touch the boolean registry (correct; this is not a boolean). Sits above it,
    at chat_f/chat_request level — exactly where the ticket said the constraint belongs.
  ADR 0005: does NOT contradict it. ADR 0005's two rewrites each produce a SINGLE-path body
    (tools->response_format, or response_format->tool); they never emit both. The bug is only
    the orthogonal case ADR 0005 never addressed: a caller passing BOTH at once. A croak refuses
    that locally instead of via an opaque provider 400 (today: `die "...request failed: 400"`,
    no body). Strictly better, non-lossy, fully reversible.
  Least-surprising: yes — fail-loud house rule, names the two legal alternatives.
  COST: the two guards are NOT identical. Cerebras = tools + response_format(any). Groq =
    tools + response_format(json_schema only) AND streaming + response_format(json_schema).
    Must read response_format.type and must fire in BOTH chat_f and chat_stream_realtime_f.
    Engine-scoped, so it is honest-but-imprecise for the future (a Cerebras model that lifts the
    limit -> false croak) and under-covers gpt-oss-120b reached via TSystems/AKIOpenAI. Neither
    is a TODAY bug: every currently-served Cerebras/Groq model rejects, so the guard is correct
    for 100% of today's models.

(b) AUTO-REWRITE (ADR 0005 direction-1 style)  —  REJECT.
  This is NOT like ADR 0005. ADR 0005 rewrites fire on a capability GAP (caller sent ONE form
  the engine can't do -> swap to the lossless equivalent). Here the caller sent TWO forms and
  the engine can do neither-together; there is NO lossless single path. Rewriting to
  "response_format only" silently drops the tools; "tools only" drops the structured final
  answer. Either way you SILENTLY DISCARD half the caller's intent — precisely the "wrong
  answer, not an error" failure ADR 0019 warns against. The correct resolution ("run tools,
  then a second structured turn" — Cerebras's own recommendation) is a TWO-turn flow that
  single-turn chat_f structurally cannot perform. So auto-rewrite is lossy and surprising here.

(c) DECLARATIVE capability_exclusions LAYER  —  REJECT for now (over- and under-powered).
  Over-built for two providers (House Rule 2). AND a flat list of exclusive flag-pairs cannot
  express the actual shapes: Groq needs mode-specificity (json_schema not json_object), a
  non-capability axis (streaming), and per-model scoping; Cerebras needs any-response_format +
  per-model. To be correct it becomes a mini constraint language. That is a genuine architecture
  decision (and directly the ADR 0019 "do not fake it in the boolean table" boundary) — Getty's
  call, not a worker's.

== 4. VERDICT — is a croak safe WITHOUT Getty's taste-call? ==

DIRECTION is safe and lands cleanly: turning the known 400 into a local croak is non-lossy,
reversible, correct for 100% of today's Cerebras/Groq models, and touches no ADR-governed seam.
(b) and (c) ARE taste-dependent — do NOT build them without Getty.

BUT even the croak embeds ONE precedent-setting choice that is Getty's to make:
  * ENGINE-scoped guard (simplest; honest "these two engines today") — a worker CAN land this
    safely as two small verified guards + a karr note deferring the rest; OR
  * MODEL-scoped guard (constraint travels with gpt-oss-120b, so it also catches TSystems/
    AKIOpenAI and self-corrects when Cerebras lifts it) — this is the market-truer design but
    needs a NEW per-model seam next to model_capability_corrections, which is exactly the k142
    architecture question ADR 0019 parked for Getty.

RECOMMENDATION for the sleeping-maintainer path: a worker may implement (a) ONLY in its
narrowest form — two engine-scoped guards, mode-aware (Groq json_schema-only) and
streaming-aware, wired into chat_f AND chat_stream_realtime_f, guarded by the canonical
predicates, worded to name the two legal alternatives — framed strictly as "convert the known
provider 400 into a local error." Everything beyond that (the model-scoped generalization, the
aggregator transitivity, any capability_exclusions framework) is deferred to Getty. If the team
would rather not bake the engine-vs-model precedent at all before he wakes, hold — the guard is
an improvement, not a fix for a live wrong-answer, so waiting costs nothing but a clearer error.

